{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"On mouse data in GEO, the NovaSeq 6000 is still the larger platform, but the X's share of 6000+X samples has risen from about 4% to about 32% in a year.","detail":"Mouse 6000 (GPL24247) vs mouse X (GPL34290 X Plus + GPL34328 X) GSM counts by submission quarter. Q3'24: 17,498 vs 713 (X share 3.9%). Q1'25: 20,534 vs 2,538 (11.0%). Q2'25: 17,754 vs 3,430 (16.2%). Q3'25: 23,719 vs 5,326 (18.3%). Q4'25: 45,314 vs 4,405 (8.9%, a 6000 spike). Q1'26: 25,326 vs 7,414 (22.7%). Q2'26: 45,530 vs 8,970 (16.4%, another 6000 spike). Q3'26: 21,245 vs 10,120 (32.3%). The mouse X share is lagging the human X share, so research conversion in the published literature is still under way. This is a trend, not a level, because GEO records submission date, not run date. Only the two large mouse X platforms were counted. The roughly 13 smaller mixed-organism X platforms add little.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24247[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12 (data through Q3'26)","source_name":"NCBI GEO via E-utilities","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"F523/F528 covered only human platforms. This is the first mouse series and the first mouse X share."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Human NovaSeq 6000 GEO samples in Q3'26 are not driven by one mega-series. The ten largest series cap out at roughly a third of the quarter.","detail":"Q3'26 human 6000 (GPL24676) had 1,647 series. By series size (n_samples counts all platforms in the series): 0 series ≥5,000; 1 series at 2,000–2,999 (GSE305971); 1 at 1,000–1,999; 8 at 500–999; 67 at 100–499. The top 10 therefore total about 7K at the lowest and about 13K at the highest, i.e. 19–36% of 36,312. Excluding them leaves at least roughly 23K human 6000 samples, so the jump is mostly broad-based. This is bounded by size filters, not exact per-GSM attribution. GSE347972 (Crnic Institute Trisomy 21 atlas, released 30 Sep 2026) has a GPL field of 24247;24676 and is a large mouse and human series, so it may add to the Q3 mouse figure.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]+AND+500:100000[n_samples]","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"This is the requested mega-series test of F528 and none of the known notes did it."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Mouse 6000 spikes in Q4'25 and Q2'26 are series-driven, so they should not be read as a trend.","detail":"Mouse 6000 samples jumped from 23,719 (Q3'25) to 45,314 (Q4'25) and from 25,326 (Q1'26) to 45,530 (Q2'26), then fell to 21,245 in Q3'26. Q4'25 had 1,295 mouse-6000 series. Nine of them had 1,000–4,999 samples (e.g. GSE181359, GSE181141, GSE181116, GSE169467), and none had 5,000 or more. Several large mid-sized atlases therefore explain the spikes. Excluding them, the underlying mouse 6000 run-rate is about 21–25K a quarter, which is flat to slightly down since Q1'25 (20.5K). The 6000's mouse deposit volume is not falling fast.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24247[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+2025/10/01:2025/12/31[PDAT]+AND+1000:4999[n_samples]&retmax=10","date":"2025-Q4 to 2026-Q3","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"This is a new unit-of-measure check (series size) on the GEO series."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Combining human and mouse, the X is still a minority of GEO 6000+X submissions in Q3'26 (about 22% on the figures pulled).","detail":"Human Q3'26: 6000 = 36,312 (F528), X (GPL34281) = 5,268, so the X share is 12.7%. Mouse Q3'26: 21,245 vs 10,120, a 32.3% share. Pooled: X = 15,388 of 72,945 = 21.1%. GEO is a lagged, academic-skewed view, compared with 83% of Illumina HT Gb shipped on the X in Q2'26. The research literature is therefore deposited from instruments or run dates a year or more earlier. It says little about current conversion but shows how long the 6000's footprint will linger in published data. The human X count comes from a single X GPL, so it may understate the true X total.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL34281[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-Q3","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"This is the first pooled human+mouse X share and uses the new mouse series."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"The mouse X count has risen about 14x in two years in published data (713 to 10,120 samples), which fits a research base that is deepening on cheaper X output.","detail":"Mouse X samples per quarter: 713 (Q3'24), 2,538 (Q1'25), 3,430, 5,326, 4,405, 7,414, 8,970, 10,120 (Q3'26). Over Q3'24 to Q3'26 that is x14.2. The 6000 count over the same span went 17,498 to 21,245 (+21%). Total mouse 6000+X samples rose from 18,211 to 31,365 (+72%) over two years. This does not support the thesis that research volume is flat. It may reflect more deposits and bigger sample counts per run.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=(GPL34290[ACCN]+OR+GPL34328[ACCN])+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-Q3","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"This is a new mouse series with arithmetic that cuts against the research volume-flat claim."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"against","claim":"GEO mouse deposits on Illumina platforms grew about 72% in two years, so published academic sequencing activity is not shrinking despite funding strain.","detail":"Total mouse 6000+X samples were 18,211 in Q3'24 and 31,365 in Q3'26 (72% higher). Q1'25 was 23,072 and Q1'26 was 32,740 (+42% y/y), a faster rate than the −7% R&A consumables drop. GEO is a lagging deposit measure, partly one-off atlases, and counts samples not Gb, so it is a weak watch-out signal.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24247[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/01/01:2026/03/31[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-Q1","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"New mouse y/y arithmetic and a contrast with R&A revenue."}
